Yield criteria, combined stress and mechanism (@mech-yield-criteria: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Plastic-flow-hardening-and-load-history.lingua#r_68855TQ5FAEA06A1AVTP8ZNZAT") { r_7FR7N6AXQPGRZZBQ57DX4FJCKR
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Yield and Plastic Flow; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 3.7 and 9.2. Distinguish yielding from fracture and from excessive elastic deflection.

For an isotropic ductile metal under the von Mises model, equivalent stress is √(((σ₁−σ₂)²+(σ₂−σ₃)²+(σ₃−σ₁)²)/2). Yield begins when this reaches the current uniaxial yield measure under the model's calibration conditions. A hydrostatic shift leaves the equivalent value unchanged. That does not imply pressure has no effect on every damage or failure mechanism.

For plane stress, σ_eq² = σ_x² − σ_xσ_y + σ_y² + 3τ_xy². The earlier 100, 20, 30 MPa example gives √11100 ≈ 105.357 MPa. Tresca instead compares the maximum principal-stress difference with a uniaxial yield measure. Neither rule automatically represents brittle ceramics, anisotropic composites or pressure-sensitive polymers.

**Work:** Compare both criteria for uniaxial tension, pure shear and biaxial loading. Use the stress at the same physical point when combining loads. Given measured material scatter and several possible service loads, identify the margin and its basis; a nominal yield ratio does not itself quantify reliability or cover fatigue, buckling or crack growth.

